3}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186318978 "Given the matrix \n$G=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(G).$"] [17592186318982 "Given the matrix \n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(F).$"] [17592186319342 "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$"] [17592186323706 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $w$ is a vector such that $w+w=w$, then $w=0$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add }(-w)\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326826 "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186326836 "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186329820 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186342108 "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$, then $3v=$"] [17592186342109 "If $w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$, then $5w=$"] [17592186342112 "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ What is $(\\frac{1}{2}a)\\cdot b?$"] [17592186342113 "Let $c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $d=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ What is $(-\\frac{1}{3}c)\\cdot d?$"] [17592186342114 "Let $v=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2x \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}3x \\\\ -3 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf $v\\cdot w=-2$ and $x>0$, find the value of $x.$"] [17592186342115 "Let $l=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ x \\\\ 2 \\\\ x \\\\ 2x\\end{bmatrix}$ and $m=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ x \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf $l\\cdot m=3$ and $x>3$, find the value of $x.$"] [17592186344968 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $x$ is a vector such that $-x+w=w$ for some $w\\in V$, then $x=0$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ x+(-x+w) & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{adding }x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (x+(-x))+w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0+w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =x+w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{adding }-w\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =x+(w+(-w)) &  & [\\,\\text{axioms  A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =x+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186344987 "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2k \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$"] [17592186345025 "Consider the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \nFind a basis $B$ of  $\\text{Col}(W).$"] [17592186345026 "The matrix $U$ is given below. Let ${u}_{1}$, ${u}_{2}$, and ${u}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $U.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{u}_{1},{u}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(U).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${u}_{2}={x}_{1}{u}_{1}+{x}_{3}{u}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]}
